Psoriasis Vulgaris (Mild Localised Plaque)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Well-demarcated erythematous plaques on elbows or knees, minimal pruritus

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-039
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of pain and bleeding from skin lesions requiring exclusion of a severe secondary bacterial infection or cellulitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate cracked, bleeding plaques on the extremities. Immediate clinical differentiation from an acute spreading cellulitis, MRSA infection, or septic joint was strictly required to determine the immediate need for intravenous antibiotic therapy prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: The patches on my elbows have cracked wide open. They are bleeding, extremely sore, and the skin around them is red and hot. I think they are infected.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a long-standing history of isolated scaly patches on extensor surfaces, diagnosed as plaque psoriasis. Due to recent dry, cold weather, the patient neglected topical treatments. Tonight, the thick plaques on the bilateral elbows fissure deeply upon bending the joint, resulting in sharp pain and minor bleeding. The patient vigorously rubbed the area, causing surrounding reactive erythema, prompting fears of a spreading bacterial infection. Denies systemic fevers, rigors, or purulent discharge.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused, in localized discomfort when moving elbows.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals well-demarcated erythematous plaques on elbows or knees, minimal pruritus historically, now acutely fissured. The plaques exhibit thick silvery scale. There are linear, bleeding cracks (fissures) within the plaques. The surrounding skin has a 1-2 cm rim of blanching erythema, consistent with mechanical irritation and inflammation, but lacks severe induration, ascending lymphangitis, fluctuance, or true calor (heat) indicative of cellulitis. Bilateral elbow joints have full range of motion without deep pain or effusions.
Environmental Observations: Environment is notably dry; advised use of a humidifier.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Has not used prescribed calcipotriol/betamethasone ointment for weeks.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Fissuring in Psoriasis Vulgaris (Mild Localised Plaque) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, absence of spreading, non-blanching erythema, and no significant localized heat or deep tissue induration.

Septic Olecranon Bursitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of a localized, fluctuant, extremely tender mass over the olecranon process, and painless passive joint movement.

Impetiginized Psoriasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of purulent, honey-colored crusting or weeping yellow exudate within the fissures.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acutely painful, bleeding cutaneous plaques with surrounding erythema, raising clinical suspicion for a secondary bacterial cellulitis or bursitis. Triage appropriately prioritized physical evaluation to prevent the potential spread of a soft tissue infection. Bedside assessment definitively excluded infectious complications, identifying the presentation as mechanical fissuring of neglected, hyperkeratotic psoriatic plaques accompanied by reactive inflammation. Providing immediate clinical reassurance, wound care advice, and averting an inappropriate emergency department presentation for intravenous antibiotics entirely satisfied the criteria for urgent after-hours intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Cleansed the fissures with sterile saline. Applied a thick layer of white soft paraffin (petroleum jelly) under a non-adherent dressing to the elbows.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply a thick layer of plain petroleum jelly over the plaques every night and wrap them loosely in a bandage before sleeping. By heavily trapping moisture overnight, you will soften the thick scales, immediately stopping the skin from cracking and bleeding when you bend your arms the next day.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 7 days to restart specific topical psoriasis therapy (e.g., steroid/vitamin D analogs).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the red area rapidly spreads down your arm, if you develop a severe, throbbing pain deep inside the elbow joint, or if you develop a fever and chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive regarding the lack of microscopic infection; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Thick psoriasis patches lose their elasticity. When the joint moves, the dry, thick skin snaps like dry leather. Softening the plaque is the only way to heal the cracks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if the cracked areas begin oozing thick yellow or green pus, or if the skin becomes hot to the touch.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The pain and bleeding are from dry cracks in the thickened skin, not a bacterial infection. The redness is just inflammation from the skin tearing.
Actionable Steps: Do not pick off the silvery scales, as this causes bleeding. Keep the elbows constantly greased with thick moisturizer. Do not bend your elbows sharply until the skin softens.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Bleeding/cracked psoriasis plaques. No signs of dangerous cellulitis or joint infection. Thick moisturizers and dressings applied. See GP next week.

SMS EDUCATION

Keep the patches heavily greased with petroleum jelly and bandaged overnight to heal the cracks. Go to ED if redness spreads rapidly or you get a fever.
